-We consider a single-server queue with Poisson arrivals, threshold-based batch formation and customer abandonment. Service times have batch-size-dependent phase-type distributions, and customer patience times are independent and exponentially distributed. A tagged-customer Markov representation yields the probabilities of service starting by a prescribed deadline, service starting after the deadline, and abandonment before service. A local error bound is obtained for truncation by the number of customers waiting behind the tagged customer. Numerical results for Erlang and hyperexponential service show that a reduction in mean waiting time among customers who enter service can accompany a decrease in the probability of timely service start among all arrivals. Cases of incorrect batch-cap selection are identified for two-moment approximations of the waiting-time distribution. In the experiments considered, preserving the zero-wait probability reduces the total number of classification errors but increases false acceptance of infeasible parameter choices.
